  "source": {
    "citation": "Kaminsky LA, Arena R, Myers J. Reference Standards for Cardiorespiratory Fitness Measured with Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing: Data from the Fitness Registry and the Importance of Exercise National Database (FRIEND). Mayo Clin Proc. 2015;90(11):1515-1523. Percentiles as tabulated in ACSM's Guidelines for Exercise Testing and Prescription, 10th ed., Table 4.7. Distance conversion: Cooper KH. A means of assessing maximal oxygen intake. JAMA. 1968;203(3):201-204.",
    "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mayocp.2015.07.026",
    "population": "US adults 20-79 who underwent maximal cardiopulmonary exercise testing on a treadmill. Note this is a self-selected, referred population and is fitter than the general public.",
    "provisional": false,
    "transcription_verified": false,
    "notes": "Two documented transformations sit between the source and these numbers, and both are worth knowing about. First, the published percentiles are VO2max in ml/kg/min; they are converted to 12-minute distance with the original Cooper regression, distance_m = 44.73 * VO2max + 504.9. That equation has a standard error of roughly 250m at the individual level, so a single Cooper result is a noisier estimate of aerobic capacity than a lab test - but the percentile ranking, which is what this app reports, is preserved because the conversion is monotone. Second, the source tabulates by decade; the 5-year bands ending in 5 are the midpoint between adjacent decades. Distances are rounded to the nearest 5m, matching the input step."
  },
